WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.060
My name is Wei Hou Wu.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.430
I'm a computer science
major and my project

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:07.250
is a model to investigate
privacy preferences,

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.970
expectations, and behaviors
of voice-activated devices.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.890
Now with the advent
of smart devices,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.040
our constitutional right
to privacy in the home

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.630
is no longer protected.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.940
That's why you have
headlines such as these.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.190
Researchers in the
past have looked

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.980
into the technical
vulnerabilities of smart device

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.900
for privacy leaks,
but they've never

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.080
looked at it from the
perspective of the consumer.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.350
The most they've done is
to look at consumer reviews

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.990
or interview consumers after
they've already used devices

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.000
for long periods of time.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.370
We seek to take
this a step further

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.860
and introduce the model
based on a variation

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.480
of in-home contextual
interviews.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.670
So what we want to do is get
voice recordings of the seconds

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.250
around when the user
activates this smart device,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.350
in this case, the Amazon Echo.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.010
To do so, we put a recording
device next to the Echo as well

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.100
as I have a whole host
of background processes

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.560
that helps in allowing
the experiment to be

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.420
within ethical guidelines.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.880
Now once we have
these recordings,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.330
we send the user a survey
containing these recordings

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.680
so that the user can replay
what the recordings contain.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.160
Now the key difference here
is that these recordings

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.530
give the user a
full picture of what

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.950
is happening at the time of
their usage of the device.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.980
As such, we can get good
informed reflections

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.660
on their usage of the device.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.830
Now we've only trialed this on
a couple of participants so far.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.920
It's far from perfect there are
still some improvements needed

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.830
for the robustness
of the setup, as well

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.970
as volume of data per day.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.210
I'm proud to say that the
model works in giving us

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.370
this unique perspective
of user privacy

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.850
preferences within the home.

